lesson "Quadratics and Factoring" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Algebra Foundations"
  difficulty: "Beginner"
  icon: "FunctionSquare"
  summary: "Roots are where the curve crosses, and factors are how you write them down. Drag the roots, watch the equation rewrite itself, then factor things that fight back."
  skills: ["alg-roots", "alg-factoring", "alg-sum-product", "alg-quadratic-formula"]

  slide "The Roots Are the Whole Story" {
    id: "a1-hook"
    cat: "Roots and Factors"
    > A quadratic that factors is really just a statement about **where it crosses
    > the x-axis**.
    >
    > If a curve crosses at $x = 2$, then $(x - 2)$ has to be one of its factors,
    > because plugging in $x = 2$ makes that bracket zero and drags the whole
    > product to zero with it. That is all a root is: a number that switches one of
    > the brackets off.
    >
    > Drag the two red crossings below and watch both forms of the equation rewrite
    > themselves to keep up.

    scene plane {
      x: [-6.5, 6.5]
      y: [-9, 11]
      grid
      axes
      param r1 = -2 { range: [-5, 5], step: 1 }
      param r2 = 3 { range: [-5, 5], step: 1 }
      curve f = (x - r1)*(x - r2) { color: primary, width: 3 }
      point p1 = (r1, 0) { drag: x -> r1, color: danger, r: 8, snap: 1 }
      point p2 = (r2, 0) { drag: x -> r2, color: danger, r: 8, snap: 1 }
      point vertex = ((r1 + r2)/2, 0 - (r2 - r1)*(r2 - r1)/4) { color: accent, r: 6 }
      label at (-6.2, 9.6) = "(x - ${r1})(x - ${r2})" { color: primary, show: r1 >= 0 and r2 >= 0 }
      label at (-6.2, 9.6) = "(x + ${0 - r1})(x - ${r2})" { color: primary, show: r1 < 0 and r2 >= 0 }
      label at (-6.2, 9.6) = "(x - ${r1})(x + ${0 - r2})" { color: primary, show: r1 >= 0 and r2 < 0 }
      label at (-6.2, 9.6) = "(x + ${0 - r1})(x + ${0 - r2})" { color: primary, show: r1 < 0 and r2 < 0 }
      label at (-6.2, 8.2) = "roots add to ${r1 + r2} and multiply to ${r1*r2}" { color: success }
      slider r1 { label: "first root" }
      slider r2 { label: "second root" }
    }

    goal "Put both roots on the negative side" {
      when: r1 < 0 and r2 < 0
      hint: "Drag both red points to the left of the origin."
    }
    goal "Now make the two roots land on top of each other" {
      when: r1 == r2
      hint: "A repeated root, where the curve just touches the axis instead of crossing it."
    }
  }

  slide "Sum and Product" {
    id: "a1-sum-product"
    cat: "Roots and Factors"
    > Look again at what the expanded form did while you were dragging. Multiply out
    > $(x - p)(x - q)$ and you get
    >
    > $$x^2 - (p + q)x + pq$$
    >
    > So in any quadratic $x^2 + bx + c$, the two roots **add** to $-b$ and
    > **multiply** to $c$. That is the actual mechanism behind "find two numbers that
    > multiply to give $c$ and add to give $b$", which you may have been doing by
    > feel.
    >
    > It also tells you where to look first. Start with the **product**, because a
    > number has only so many factor pairs, and then check which pair adds up right.

    numeric {
      ask "The quadratic $x^2 - 7x + 12$ factors as $(x - p)(x - q)$. What is $p \times q$?"
      skill: "alg-sum-product"
      answer: 12
      expect: 12
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "The product of the roots is the constant term, sitting right there at the end."
      ! "$12$, and the sum is $7$. The pair that does both is $3$ and $4$, so it factors as $(x-3)(x-4)$."
    }
  }

  slide "Factor It" {
    id: "a1-build"
    cat: "Roots and Factors"
    build {
      ask "Factor $x^2 + 5x + 6$."
      skill: "alg-factoring"
      reusable
      bank: ["(", ")", "x", "+", "2", "3", "-"]
      answer: ["(", "x", "+", "2", ")", "(", "x", "+", "3", ")"]
      hint "Which two numbers multiply to $6$ and add to $5$?"
      ! "$2 \times 3 = 6$ and $2 + 3 = 5$, so $(x+2)(x+3)$. The roots are $-2$ and $-3$: the signs flip when you read them out of the brackets."
    }
  }

  slide "Watch the Signs" {
    id: "a1-signs"
    cat: "Roots and Factors"
    quiz {
      ask "What are the roots of $(x + 4)(x - 7) = 0$?"
      skill: "alg-roots"
      * "$x = -4$ and $x = 7$"
      - "$x = 4$ and $x = -7$" { why: "The signs flip when you read roots off brackets. $(x+4)$ is zero when $x = -4$, not $+4$." }
      - "$x = 4$ and $x = 7$" { why: "Only the second one. $(x+4)$ needs $x = -4$ to vanish." }
      hint "Ask what value of $x$ makes each bracket equal zero."
      ! "Set each bracket to zero on its own. $x + 4 = 0$ gives $x = -4$; $x - 7 = 0$ gives $x = 7$."
    }
  }

  slide "When the x² Has a Coefficient" {
    id: "a1-harder"
    cat: "Harder Polynomials"
    > Everything so far assumed the $x^2$ came alone. Once there is a number in
    > front, like $2x^2 + 7x + 3$, the trick has to grow slightly.
    >
    > Now you need two numbers that multiply to $a \times c$ — here $2 \times 3 = 6$
    > — and still add to $b = 7$. That pair is $6$ and $1$. Split the middle term
    > with them and factor in pairs:
    >
    > $$2x^2 + 7x + 3 = 2x^2 + 6x + x + 3 = 2x(x + 3) + 1(x + 3) = (2x + 1)(x + 3)$$
    >
    > The $(x+3)$ appearing in both halves is the signal that it has worked. If your
    > two halves do not share a bracket, you split the middle term with the wrong
    > pair.

    order {
      ask "Put the steps in order for factoring $3x^2 + 10x + 8$."
      skill: "alg-factoring"
      item "Multiply $a \times c$: $3 \times 8 = 24$"
      item "Find two numbers multiplying to $24$ and adding to $10$: that is $6$ and $4$"
      item "Split the middle: $3x^2 + 6x + 4x + 8$"
      item "Factor each pair: $3x(x + 2) + 4(x + 2)$"
      item "Collect the shared bracket: $(3x + 4)(x + 2)$"
      decoy "Find two numbers multiplying to $8$ and adding to $10$"
      ! "The decoy is the old method, and with a coefficient on $x^2$ it simply fails: nothing multiplies to $8$ and adds to $10$. You have to use $a \times c$."
    }
  }

  slide "When Nothing Factors" {
    id: "a1-formula"
    cat: "Harder Polynomials"
    > Plenty of quadratics have perfectly good roots that are not whole numbers, and
    > no amount of staring will factor them. For those, the formula always works:
    >
    > $$x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$$
    >
    > The interesting part is under the square root. $b^2 - 4ac$ is called the
    > **discriminant**, and it tells you the shape of the answer before you finish:
    >
    > **Positive** means two distinct roots and the curve cuts the axis twice.
    > **Zero** means one repeated root and the curve just touches. **Negative** means
    > no real roots at all and the curve misses the axis completely.
    >
    > Slide the curve up and down and watch the discriminant decide.

    scene plane {
      x: [-5.5, 5.5]
      y: [-6, 10]
      grid
      axes
      param c = -3 { range: [-4, 6], step: 0.5 }
      curve f = x*x - 2*x + c { color: primary, width: 3 }
      label at (-5.2, 8.6) = "x² - 2x + ${c}" { color: primary }
      label at (-5.2, 7.2) = "discriminant = ${4 - 4*c}" { color: accent }
      label at (-5.2, 5.8) = "two roots" { color: success, show: 4 - 4*c > 0 }
      label at (-5.2, 5.8) = "one repeated root" { color: warning, show: 4 - 4*c == 0 }
      label at (-5.2, 5.8) = "no real roots" { color: danger, show: 4 - 4*c < 0 }
      slider c { label: "constant term c" }
    }

    goal "Lift the curve until it has no real roots at all" {
      when: 4 - 4*c < 0
      hint: "Raise c until the whole parabola clears the x-axis."
    }
  }

  slide "Read the Discriminant" {
    id: "a1-discriminant"
    cat: "Harder Polynomials"
    sort {
      ask "Sort each quadratic by how many real roots it has."
      skill: "alg-quadratic-formula"
      bin "Two real roots": ["$x^2 - 5x + 6$", "$x^2 - 4$"]
      bin "One repeated root": ["$x^2 - 6x + 9$", "$x^2 + 4x + 4$"]
      bin "No real roots": ["$x^2 + x + 5$", "$x^2 + 9$"]
      hint "Work out $b^2 - 4ac$ for each and look only at its sign."
      ! "The discriminants are $1$, $16$, $0$, $0$, $-19$ and $-36$. You never had to find a single root to sort them, which is exactly what the discriminant is for."
    }
  }

  slide "All Together" {
    id: "a1-synth"
    cat: "Harder Polynomials"
    numeric {
      ask "Solve $2x^2 - 7x + 3 = 0$ and give the **larger** root."
      skill: "alg-quadratic-formula"
      answer: 3
      expect: (7 + sqrt(49 - 24))/4
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Try factoring first: you need two numbers multiplying to $2 \times 3 = 6$ and adding to $-7$."
      hint "That pair is $-6$ and $-1$, giving $(2x - 1)(x - 3)$."
      ! "$(2x-1)(x-3) = 0$, so $x = \tfrac12$ or $x = 3$. The formula agrees: $b^2-4ac = 49-24 = 25$, and $\sqrt{25} = 5$ is a whole number, which is precisely why it factored."
    }
  }
}
